---
import ResourceLayout from '../../components/ResourceLayout.astro';
---

<ResourceLayout
  icon="📘"
  eyebrow="Self-paced learning module"
  title="How Healthcare Works in BC"
  introduction="Work through this plain-language module to learn how healthcare services are organized in British Columbia and how to choose where to go for help."
>
  <section>
    <h2>1. Start with the right service</h2>
    <p>
      Family doctors, nurse practitioners, walk-in clinics, Urgent and Primary
      Care Centres, pharmacists, 8-1-1, and emergency departments each have
      different roles. Choosing the right service can help you receive care
      sooner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Prepare for your visit</h2>
    <p>
      Bring your BC Services Card or other coverage information, a list of
      medications, and the questions you want to ask. You can request an
      interpreter when you book or attend an appointment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Understand follow-up</h2>
    <p>
      Ask what happens next, when you should expect test results, who will
      contact you, and where to go if your symptoms change. Write down the
      name of the clinic or provider and any follow-up date.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Ask for help navigating the system</h2>
    <p>
      HealthLink BC at 8-1-1 can connect you with a nurse, pharmacist,
      dietitian, or health service navigator. Settlement organizations can also
      help newcomers understand services, forms, language support, and coverage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <aside class="notice">
    <strong>Remember</strong>
    <p>
      This module is for general education. Call 911 or go to the nearest
      emergency department for a life-threatening emergency.
    </p>
  </aside>
</ResourceLayout>
